grammar_rules_find_error.explanation:
Passive voice: imperative sentences, command and order,
object given - let + obj + be + Past Participle;
no object - you are ordered/commanded to.

Active/Pasive voice: 
imperative sentences: permission, request, advice;
object given: passive: obj + should + be + Past participle;
no object: passive: you are allowed, requested, or advised to....

Adverbs of manner:
- intransitive verb goes before the adverb;
- transitive verb goes either before or after the adverb.

A preposition is usually placed before its object but sometimes it's placed after it in the following cases:
- interrogative pronoun;
- when object of the preposition is a relative pronoun 'that';
- when an infinitive qualifies a noun, the preposition should be placed after the infinitive.

Change of this, there:
- used with time, change into 'that', 'those': this week -> that week, this month -> that month;
- used as adjective, changes into 'the': this book -> the book, these books -> the books;
- used as pronoun, changes into 'it': I shall do this tomorrow -> I shall do it tomorrow;
- used as subject, does not change 'this', 'these', give the sense of the object being near: this is my book, these are my books.

If there is a feeling of suggestion, then following construction is used:
Active: v1 + O;
Passive: 1. Sub + should + be + V3.

Exclamatory sentence: 
said+ alas! > exclaimed with sorrow; 
said + hurray > exlaimed with joy; 
said + fi!/ugh! > exclaimed with despise/disgust; 
said + oh! > exclaimed with surprise/regret; 
said + bravo! > applaud.

Wrong noun combinations - correction form:
Cousin brother / Cousin sister - Cousin;
Pick Pocketer - Pick Pocket;
Good name - Name;
Big Blunder - Blunder (means a big mistake);
Strong Breeze- Strong Wind (Breeze is laways light and gentle);
Bad dream - Nightmare;
Proudy - Proud;
According to me - In my opinion.
